KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ pair<const int,int> is_constructible from pair<int,int>&&: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [meta.unary.prop], [temp.deduct.call]/4, [temp.deduct.type]:
is_constructible<pair<const int,int>, pair<int,int>&&> is true (g++/clang++
agree) -- the converting constructor template pair(pair<U1,U2>&&) deduces
U1=U2=int and binds the rvalue.  CBMC reports false: deducing the constructor
template's parameters from the argument deduces U1=int but leaves U2 unassigned,
so the enable_if non-type parameter's condition (which mentions U2) cannot be
substituted, typecheck_type of the parameter type throws, and the candidate is
dropped ("found no match for symbol 'pair'").  user_defined_conversion_sequence
correctly delegates the template converting constructor to new_temporary; the
defect is in the constructor-template argument deduction.  Root of std::map /
std::unordered_map insert(_Pair&&) overload selection for a convertible pair
(dog-food: expr.cpp, expr_util.cpp, pointer_predicates.cpp,
irep_serialization.cpp).  Reproduces only with libstdc++'s full pair machinery
(minimal hand-written converting ctors deduce both parameters), hence the header
dependency.  Flip to CORE once deduction assigns every constructor template
parameter for this source.
